      'Every turn covers more time than the last. The first turn of the Dusk is 40 years; by its end a turn is about a trillion years. The Chronometer across the top shows where you are in the remaining life of the universe, with the milestones and your forecasts marked on it.',
      'Pace (bottom right) sets the length of the coming turns, counted in turns of the Tide, the natural pace of the age: at Quick ×10 ten turns pass for every turn at the Tide, at Slow ×10 one turn for ten. A turn gathers and wears your worlds by the turns of the Tide it covers, so over any stretch of time they come to the same at any pace; its machines and sleepers cost what it covers too, but a whole turn of the Tide at least, so quickening costs upkeep. Quick gives you more decisions while something bright is burning; Slow fewer, with the universe moving on further between them, and only minds that can slow their own clocks (Echoes, Coldminds) avoid paying for the extra time. How far you can go either way grows with research (Hibernation Protocols slows, Quickening quickens); letting the Prophet of Stillness’s idea spread lets you slow one step further still. No slow turn runs on to the end of an age: near its end a slow pace is greyed out, and the Tide or a quicker pace carries you across. No turn runs past the end of its age, or the Great Evaporation if space unmakes matter: one that would stops there, paid for the time it covered. The Dark Era keeps a clock of its own: there a pace only halves or doubles the turn, paying for what it covers, so the dark always takes many turns. A pace that would change nothing at the moment, or may not be taken, is greyed out and says why: while a new star’s clock or a flare sets your turns, when a flare will cut the turn short whatever the pace, when a slow turn would reach the end of the age, or when the turn would stop at its end either way. The pace in use is lit; one you chose that is greyed for now keeps an outline and comes back by itself.',
