      'Trip times read like “~26t · 2.9 Myr”: turns at your current pace, then the cosmic time the flight takes. Ships never outrun light (a probe flies at 2% of it), but every turn is longer than the last, so a trip across the Coalescence needs far fewer turns than distance ÷ today’s turn length suggests. Quicken and the same flight takes more, shorter turns; slow down and it takes fewer. Choose on map (in a fleet’s panel or an idle ship’s card) sends a ship to any star you click; the banner shows the trip before you commit, and Esc cancels.',
      'Auto-explore (probes and other survey ships): the ship keeps charting on its own, always heading for the nearest star no one has surveyed and no other ship is bound for. It never takes the last of the reserve (it waits while launching would leave under 20 energy), stops asking for orders, and reports back when nothing is left to chart. Any order by hand, Hold, or Stop exploring ends it.',
      'When a ship runs out of orders, a card asks what next: the nearest unsurveyed stars (for a probe; other ships get the two nearest as well), or Fortify for warships. Fortified warships defend their system at double strength (an Aegis triple; not while standing off) and turn raiders away from the capital; Hold parks any other ship. Neither is counted as idle again until you give it new orders.',
      'Warships come in kinds, each with its own strength against ships, settlements and swarms, its hull and its upkeep (the build list gives the figures). The Picket is light and cheap: it sees twice as far from where it waits, and turns half the raids away from its star. The Warden holds the line. The Hunter, once Hunger Studies are known, strikes a swarm twice as hard as a Warden. The Aegis is heavy, and strongest dug in. The Breacher, once Aegis Lattices are known, is a siege hull: without one, a settlement behind a Defence Grid or a Bastion Shell cannot be stormed. The Kinetic Lance, once Relativistic Arks are known, is one strike at an enemy’s settlement from anywhere (see War). Every warship costs energy a turn to keep (more at a pace slower than the Tide, a tenth while you sleep; Disband one you no longer need, where it waits, for some of its matter), and a Shipyard mends a tenth of a hull each turn for every ship stopped at its star. Screens add up: each ship at a star has its own chance of turning a raid away.',
      'Every fleet with a warship fights by a tactic, in every fight it is in, against ships, settlements and swarms, until you change it (Tactic, on its panel; the fleet list shows any but Hold the line). Hold the line: as every fleet fought before. Close in: its ships deal half again as much and take half again as much. Between two fleets that only ends the fight sooner, so there is less time for help to come and luck counts for more; but the guns of a settlement, which no battle silences, hit it harder too, while against a swarm it is likelier to win. Stand off: half the fire both ways, so a fight lasts twice as long, for holding out or waiting for help; it holds no siege, cannot storm or raid, and is not dug in. Escort: its warships fire a quarter less, but take the fire meant for its unarmed ships (settlers and probes are otherwise hit first). With an enemy at the fleet’s star, each tactic’s button says what it would deal and take there this turn, at an even draw.',
      'Collision stars (two brown dwarfs, each too small to burn hydrogen, collided and merged into a body heavy enough to burn it) are the brightest places of the Degenerate Age: while one burns on your map it heads every list of destinations, settlements and worlds, and its name shows on the galaxy map with a ✦. In this age the Systems window has a third tab, Collision stars, listing each one with the light it gives and the turns it has left at your pace. A turn here soon outlasts a collision star’s whole life (one to ten trillion years), and a helium star’s (two white dwarfs merged, a few hundred million years) many times over. So every new star lights as the turn it forms in ends (the game bends this: the two may have met at any moment of that turn), and its event lets you keep time with it, as with a flare: what is left of its life in six turns, whatever your pace, each lived in full and paying as one turn at the Tide, with the Pace panel counting them. That is free while your next turn is no more than a hundred times what is left of its life. Beyond that your minds must quicken to it, which costs 250 energy, once, for every further tenfold, though never more than a full store: a quicker pace pays less, a slower one more, and a White Fire costs everyone something, usually all the energy they hold. A star too brief for six turns (a helium giant’s 120,000 years, or late in the age a helium star) can still be caught in one, a flash, priced the same way: one turn that ends as it burns out (or, past about η 18, after the shortest turn the calendar can count, its light still lived in full). One clock at a time, but a new star that outlasts the one you keep time with can be followed on: its six turns begin as the first clock runs out, at the price it would cost from your own pace then and never less than 250 energy (a new star lights about as often as one goes out, so a clock passed on cheaply would hold the age still). The Pace panel names the star that waits. With Quickening, Quick ×10 works while you keep time too: each of the star’s turns splits into ten, each lived as a tenth of a Tide turn, so it gives the same light over ten times the turns, and every turn pays its upkeep. Whatever your pace you can study a new star instead, for insight and resolve, and in its light you find its worlds, so its system is charted. One new star, the rarest of all, is free to keep time with whatever your pace. A helium star’s ultraviolet boils away the worlds closest to it (any whose warmest ground passes 3,000 K, and rubble from 1,600 K; gas and ice giants are spared) before the turn ends: its event names them, and our people there flee to the Deep, three in four of them. A heavier one swells into a helium giant some 25 times the Sun’s size and swallows whatever orbits inside it. Let one pass, and late in the age it comes and goes inside your next turn.',
      'Falling worlds (in a game begun since 20 October): a world of rock or ice that reaches its dead star’s tidal limit is torn into a ring of rubble, twice as rich to mine as the world was and thinning as it rains in; A World Falls lets you evacuate it or stay and mine it. A black hole takes such a ring within the turn, a faint flash that harms nothing: each Burst Catcher in its system banks 250, as do those out to about 2 light-years, less beyond. A giant falls in a flash at its star’s limit: its forecast gives the date, how far out the ground melts and what each of your settlements there needs. Inside that line a settlement on a tidally locked world lives through it with a Flash Shelter (Deep Warrens or Night-side Shelters do as well); one on a turning world, on a belt, in a giant’s clouds or in the Deep needs a Shadow Shield; nothing on a world the flash boils away can be saved. Beyond the line nothing is harmed, and Burst Catchers bank the flash much farther off (about a light-year from a white dwarf’s, forty from a black hole’s). The Flash Shelter comes with the Deep Warrens research, the Shadow Shield with Orbital Industry, both from the Dusk: a white dwarf’s close giant can fall in before the Last Light. A black hole’s panel gives its Disk luminosity in Suns.',
      'Think ahead: after the Last Light the useful places are white dwarfs warmed by dark matter (for ember collectors) and black holes (for accretion and, later, Penrose and Hawking harvesting).',
